  {
   "id": "7d9d74a5-9318-4ac8-b2c1-522a727b6cdc",
   "term": "Partial popping",
   "fields": {
    "definition": "On a brass (lip-reed) instrument, the unintended sounding of a neighboring partial of the harmonic series - the note momentarily \"pops\" to the harmonic above or below the intended one because the lips lock onto the wrong resonance. It produces a cracked or split note, most common in the upper register where partials sit close together.",
    "plain_english": "When a trumpet or horn player accidentally hits the wrong note in the natural series, so the note jumps or cracks. It happens because the nearby notes are easy to slip onto, especially up high.",
    "purpose_function": "Understanding partial popping helps players and techs know why brass notes crack and how lip tension and air support keep the intended harmonic locked in.",
    "practical_application": "A technician or player traces cracked notes to lip setup, air support, or mouthpiece and tuning issues rather than assuming the instrument is faulty.",
    "category": "Brass technique",
    "related_terms": ["Harmonic series", "Partial", "Embouchure", "Lip reed", "Cracked note", "Register"],
    "common_mistakes": ["Blaming the instrument for popped partials that come from embouchure or air support.", "Expecting clean upper-register notes without enough breath support.", "Confusing a popped partial with a valve or slide error."],
    "scenario_contexts": ["A trumpet player cracking a high note as it pops to the next partial.", "A horn section where closely spaced partials make entrances risky.", "Reviewing a brass take and hearing a note split to a neighboring harmonic."]
   },
   "sources": ["Fletcher & Rossing, The Physics of Musical Instruments (Springer) - brass instruments, playing the harmonic series and partials", "Baines, Brass Instruments: Their History and Development"],
   "confidence": "Medium",
   "flags": []
  },
  {
   "id": "ed37c62f-f77e-4595-9a33-35799e8b830a",
   "term": "Pipe hiss",
   "fields": {
    "definition": "A steady, broadband high-frequency hiss radiated by an organ flue pipe, produced by turbulent air where the wind jet crosses the pipe's mouth and upper lip, accompanying its tone. Excess hiss can indicate over-high wind pressure, a maladjusted jet, or voicing that emphasizes air noise over harmonic tone.",
    "plain_english": "The airy, hissing sound that comes from an organ pipe along with its note, made by wind rushing across the pipe's mouth. It is like the breathy hiss you hear when blowing hard across a bottle top.",
    "purpose_function": "A little jet hiss is built into how a flue pipe makes sound, so judging it helps voicers and recordists tell healthy speech from noisy, poorly regulated pipes.",
    "practical_application": "A voicer adjusts wind pressure and the pipe's mouth (cut-up and jet) to balance tone against hiss, while a recordist places mics so as not to over-emphasize pipe hiss on soft stops.",
    "category": "Organ noise",
    "related_terms": ["Flue pipe", "Organ wind noise", "Air jet", "Cut-up", "Wind pressure", "Voicing"],
    "common_mistakes": ["Treating all pipe hiss as a fault when some jet noise is natural to flue speech.", "Raising wind pressure for more volume and adding harsh hiss.", "Boosting treble in the mix and exaggerating pipe hiss on quiet passages."],
    "scenario_contexts": ["A softly voiced flute stop whose jet hiss is audible up close.", "A voicer reducing hiss by refining the cut-up and wind pressure.", "Recording a quiet registration where pipe hiss competes with the tone."]
   },
   "sources": ["Fletcher & Rossing, The Physics of Musical Instruments (Springer) - flue-pipe air jet, edge tone and mouth", "Audsley, The Art of Organ-Building - voicing, cut-up and wind pressure", "Grove Dictionary of Music, 'Organ' (flue-pipe voicing)"],
